From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 08:20:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.security.authentication.onlineid: a WAM broker Office
 will talk to.

Office 365 signs in through WebAuthenticationCoreManager. Wine has the
runtime class registered to nothing, so every call failed and Office
concluded the broker was broken. This adds the two statics interfaces it
asks for, the objects they hand back, and the WebTokenRequest class, and
routes both runtime classes to this dll from combase until classes.idl
carries the registration.

The rule the whole file follows is the one sppc already cost a day to
learn: refusing a call and answering it are not the same thing to the
caller. Every method here answers. Measured over one Word start-up, with
each change made only after the log said Office was asking:

  FindAccountAsync            E_NOTIMPL -> a completed operation. On
      E_NOTIMPL Office stopped after 19 calls; answering moved it onto
      the whole token path (GetTokenSilently 0 -> 20, request
      properties 0 -> 203).
  FindAccountAsync            "no such account" -> the account, when the
      id asked for is the CID this prefix holds. Office asks 15 times
      per start-up about the id it wrote when it last licensed, and a
      broker that mints tokens for an account it says it does not have
      is not consistent enough to be believed.
  FindAllAccounts*            empty list -> that same account, for the
      same reason: 35 calls were told there were no accounts in a run
      that took 16 tokens for one.
  FindSystemAccountProvider*  E_NOTIMPL -> the provider its non-system
      siblings already return, which reports IsSystemProvider TRUE.
  RequestToken*               E_NOTIMPL -> the held token, or UserCancel.
      This broker cannot put a sign-in window on the screen, and
      UserInteractionRequired would ask the caller to do the thing it
      just asked us to do.
  InvalidateCacheAsync        E_NOTIMPL -> a completed IAsyncAction.
  WebTokenResponse.Properties NULL -> an empty map. NULL is not "no
      properties", it is a pointer the caller has to guess is absent.

Two things had to be right that were not obvious:

IAsyncAction and IAsyncOperation<T> share every vtable slot but the
last, where GetResults takes no out parameter. Returning an action
through the operation vtable makes GetResults write through whatever is
in the second argument register, so the operation object carries a flag
and returns early.

A WinRT map is iterable, and that is not an optional extra. Office asks
the property bag for IIterable<IKeyValuePair<String,String>> the moment
it is handed one; answering E_NOINTERFACE there took Word down with an
unhandled 0xe0000002. IIterable, IIterator and IKeyValuePair are
implemented over the existing entries.

Tokens are looked up per scope, under Z:\tmp\office-wam-tokens, because
Office does not ask for "a token": one start-up makes 16 requests, ten
of them for service::officeapps.live.com::MBI_SSL_SHORT and the rest for
graph, the consent service, ads and ssl.live.com. Handing one string
back for all of them is a fixed answer shaped like a token. The
single-file form is still read when that directory is absent.

After this the whole WAM surface Office touches answers: no FIXME is
reached in a full start-up and nothing crashes. It does not license
Office -- see docs/office365-under-wine.md for what that measurement
then ruled out -- but it is the sign-in stack behaving, which every
later attempt needs underneath it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
